METHODOLOGY_3_1 = """The architectural design of the proposed plagiarism detection system follows the microservices paradigm, a software development approach that structures applications as collections of loosely coupled, independently deployable services (Newman, 2015). This architectural choice is motivated by several factors critical to plagiarism detection systems: the need for independent scaling of computationally intensive components, the ability to update machine learning models without system-wide deployments, and the isolation of failure domains to prevent cascading failures.

The system comprises three primary services communicating through well-defined RESTful APIs:

The Frontend Service, implemented in React 18 with TypeScript, provides the user interface layer. React's component-based architecture enables modular development of complex user interfaces, while TypeScript adds static type checking that catches errors at compile time rather than runtime (Bierman et al., 2014). The frontend employs Zustand for lightweight state management, chosen over Redux for its simpler API and reduced boilerplate, and React Query for server state synchronization with built-in caching, background refetching, and optimistic updates.

The Backend Service, built on Node.js with Express.js, serves as the API gateway and orchestration layer. Node.js's event-driven, non-blocking I/O model is particularly suited for I/O-intensive operations such as database queries and inter-service communication (Tilkov and Vinoski, 2010). The service implements JWT-based authentication with access and refresh token patterns, providing stateless authentication suitable for horizontal scaling while maintaining security through short-lived access tokens and server-side refresh token validation.

The AI Service, developed in Python using the FastAPI framework, houses the core intelligence of the system. Python's dominance in the machine learning ecosystem provides access to mature libraries including PyTorch, Transformers, and Sentence Transformers. FastAPI was selected over alternatives like Flask for its automatic API documentation generation, built-in request validation using Pydantic models, and native async support enabling efficient handling of concurrent requests (Ramirez, 2019).

Data persistence utilizes PostgreSQL as the primary relational database, selected for its robust ACID compliance, advanced indexing capabilities, and excellent performance characteristics for mixed read-write workloads (Stonebraker and Hellerstein, 1998). Redis provides caching and session storage, leveraging its in-memory data structure store for sub-millisecond response times on frequently accessed data.

The containerization strategy employs Docker for packaging services with their dependencies into portable, reproducible units. Docker Compose orchestrates the multi-container deployment for development environments, while the production deployment on Railway provides managed hosting with automatic scaling, integrated logging, and zero-downtime deployments."""

METHODOLOGY_3_2 = """Document processing transforms raw uploaded files into structured representations suitable for semantic analysis. This pipeline implements a series of carefully designed transformations, each addressing specific challenges in text extraction and normalization.

Text Extraction constitutes the first transformation, converting binary file formats to plain text while preserving semantic content. For PDF files, the system employs pdfplumber, a Python library that provides advanced PDF parsing capabilities including layout analysis and table extraction. Unlike simpler alternatives that extract text sequentially, pdfplumber maintains reading order by analyzing the spatial positioning of text elements (Fitz, 2021). Edge cases require special handling: OCR-based PDFs containing scanned images require integration with Tesseract for optical character recognition; password-protected documents are rejected with appropriate error messages; and malformed headers trigger fallback parsing strategies.

DOCX processing utilizes the python-docx library to traverse the Open XML document structure. The implementation extracts text from paragraphs, tables, headers, footers, and text boxes while preserving structural boundaries that inform chunking decisions. Importantly, tracked changes and comments are excluded to analyze only the final document content, preventing draft material from affecting similarity scores.

Plain text files undergo encoding detection using the chardet library before UTF-8 normalization, ensuring consistent handling of documents created across different systems and locales.

Text Normalization addresses the substantial variation in text representation that can artificially inflate or deflate similarity scores. The normalization pipeline applies the following transformations in sequence: Unicode normalization (NFKC form) standardizes equivalent representations, particularly for accented characters and ligatures; whitespace normalization collapses multiple spaces, tabs, and newlines to single spaces; case preservation maintains original casing, as aggressive lowercasing loses important distinction between proper nouns and common words; punctuation normalization standardizes quotation marks, dashes, and ellipses across different typographic conventions; and special character filtering removes non-printable characters while preserving semantic punctuation.

Text Chunking segments normalized documents into overlapping windows for embedding generation. The chunking strategy represents a crucial design decision balancing competing concerns: larger chunks capture more context but produce more general embeddings; smaller chunks enable precise matching but may miss semantic units that span boundaries. Following empirical analysis and guidance from Reimers and Gurevych (2019), the system employs 300-word chunks with 50-word overlap.

The overlap strategy ensures that semantic units falling near chunk boundaries appear in full in at least one chunk. The mathematical formulation specifies that for a document of n words, with chunk size c and overlap o, the number of chunks generated is ceil((n - o) / (c - o)). Each chunk i starts at position (c - o) * i and extends to min((c - o) * i + c, n)."""

METHODOLOGY_3_3 = """The transformation of text chunks to dense vector representations constitutes the core enabling technology for semantic similarity detection. The system employs the all-MiniLM-L6-v2 model from the Sentence Transformers library, a choice informed by rigorous analysis of available alternatives.

The all-MiniLM-L6-v2 model represents a distilled version of the MiniLM architecture (Wang et al., 2020), which itself derives from knowledge distillation of BERT (Hinton et al., 2015). The model contains 22.7 million parameters across 6 transformer layers, producing 384-dimensional embeddings. Compared to larger alternatives like MPNet (420M parameters, 768 dimensions) or RoBERTa-large (355M parameters, 1024 dimensions), MiniLM-L6-v2 offers a compelling tradeoff: semantic textual similarity benchmarks show only a 2-3% accuracy reduction while inference speed improves by 5x and memory requirements decrease by 4x.

The embedding generation process transforms each text chunk through the following steps: Tokenization converts text to WordPiece tokens with a vocabulary of 30,522 tokens, handling out-of-vocabulary words through subword decomposition; Transformer encoding processes tokens through 6 attention layers, each applying multi-head self-attention followed by position-wise feed-forward networks; Mean pooling aggregates token embeddings into a single sentence embedding by computing the mean of token vectors weighted by attention masks; and L2 normalization scales embedding vectors to unit length, simplifying similarity computation to dot products.

The mathematical formulation of mean pooling is expressed as: for token embeddings T = [t1, t2, ..., tn] and attention mask M = [m1, m2, ..., mn], the sentence embedding S equals the sum of (ti * mi) for all i, divided by the sum of mi for all i. This formulation correctly handles padding tokens by zeroing their contribution through the attention mask.

Normalization to unit vectors has important implications for similarity computation. For normalized vectors u and v, cosine similarity equals their dot product: cos(u, v) = u · v. This simplification enables use of highly optimized linear algebra routines for similarity computation, achieving significant speedups compared to explicit cosine similarity calculations."""

METHODOLOGY_3_4 = """The efficient storage and retrieval of embedding vectors requires specialized data structures optimized for high-dimensional similarity search. The system employs FAISS (Facebook AI Similarity Search), implementing an exact L2 search index for maximum precision in plagiarism detection where false negatives carry significant cost.

The choice of IndexFlatL2 for exact search, rather than approximate indices, merits justification. Approximate methods introduce recall loss: some true nearest neighbors may be missed due to the approximation. In plagiarism detection, a missed match could allow plagiarized content to pass undetected, a false negative with potentially serious consequences. Analysis indicates that for indices up to 10 million vectors, exact search remains computationally feasible with query times under 1 second on modern hardware.

The L2 (Euclidean) distance metric for normalized vectors relates directly to cosine similarity through the identity: ||u - v||^2 = 2 - 2*cos(u, v). Thus, minimizing L2 distance is equivalent to maximizing cosine similarity for normalized embeddings, and FAISS's L2 index correctly identifies semantically similar texts.

Index construction proceeds incrementally as documents are ingested. Upon receiving embeddings for a new document, the system computes the current index size, adds new vectors using faiss.IndexFlatL2.add(), updates the metadata store with document-to-vector mappings, and persists both index and metadata to disk for durability.

Metadata management employs a parallel array structure where each vector index corresponds to an entry in the metadata list containing document_id (unique document identifier linking to the relational database), user_id (owner identifier for access control), chunk_index (position within the source document), chunk_text (original text for result display), filename (human-readable identifier), sentiment (pre-computed sentiment analysis result), context (extracted keywords and topics), and summary (generated document summary). This design enables efficient lookup of document details given a FAISS search result, supporting rich result presentation without additional database queries.

Persistence utilizes FAISS's native serialization for the vector index and Python's pickle for metadata. The atomic update pattern writes to temporary files before renaming to final paths, preventing corruption from interrupted write operations. Recovery logic reconstructs metadata from the relational database if pickle files become corrupted."""

METHODOLOGY_3_5 = """The weighted mean scoring algorithm represents a novel contribution addressing fundamental limitations of naive averaging approaches to similarity aggregation. The algorithm ensures that substantial matches in longer text passages contribute proportionally more to overall scores than incidental matches in short phrases.

The mathematical formulation defines the weighted similarity score. For a query document Q chunked into segments {q1, q2, ..., qm}, each with length len(qi) and maximum similarity to any indexed document si = max_j(similarity(qi, dj)), the weighted overall score equals the sum of len(qi) * si for all i, divided by the sum of len(qi) for all i. This formulation ensures that a 200-word passage matching at 90% similarity contributes four times as much as a 50-word passage matching at the same level.

Per-document scores follow an analogous formulation, restricting the maximum operation to chunks from a specific source document. This enables users to understand not just overall similarity but which specific sources contribute most significantly to the aggregate score.

The similarity threshold parameter (default 0.88) filters matches before aggregation. This threshold was empirically tuned using a development dataset of known plagiarism cases: thresholds below 0.85 produced excessive false positives from topically similar but independently authored content; thresholds above 0.92 missed legitimate paraphrasing detection opportunities. The 0.88 value optimizes the F1 score balancing precision and recall.

Distance-to-similarity conversion transforms FAISS L2 distances to the [0, 1] similarity scale. For normalized vectors, L2 distances range from 0 (identical) to 2 (orthogonal). The conversion formula similarity = 1 - (distance / 2) maps this range to similarities from 1 (identical) to 0 (orthogonal).

Deduplication logic addresses potential double-counting when the same source document appears multiple times due to re-indexing or versioning. The algorithm groups matches by filename, retaining only the version with the highest weighted similarity score for each unique filename in the final results."""

METHODOLOGY_3_6 = """The multi-model analysis pipeline enriches similarity results with contextual information from three complementary analysis modalities: sentiment analysis, keyword extraction, and document summarization. These analyses provide users with deeper understanding of detected similarities and their potential implications.

Sentiment Analysis employs a fine-tuned DistilBERT model (distilbert-base-uncased-finetuned-sst-2-english) to classify document tone. DistilBERT (Sanh et al., 2019) applies knowledge distillation to reduce BERT's 110M parameters to 66M while retaining 97% of language understanding capability. The SST-2 fine-tuning trains on the Stanford Sentiment Treebank (Socher et al., 2013), a benchmark dataset of movie review sentiment.

The sentiment classification informs plagiarism analysis in several ways: matching documents with opposing sentiments may indicate that similar content is being used to make opposite arguments, suggesting independent authorship; consistent sentiment across matches reinforces the likelihood of direct derivation; and unusual sentiment in academic documents may flag inappropriate sources.

Keyword Extraction utilizes KeyBERT (Grootendorst, 2020), which combines BERT embeddings with maximal marginal relevance to extract diverse, representative keywords. The algorithm embeds both the full document and candidate keywords (noun phrases extracted via spaCy), then selects keywords that maximize similarity to the document while minimizing redundancy among selected keywords.

The extracted keywords serve multiple purposes: identifying the primary topics of compared documents, enabling topic-based filtering of similarity results, highlighting domain-specific terminology that may legitimately appear across independent works, and providing vocabulary for understanding matching passages without reading full texts.

Document Summarization employs DistilBART (Shleifer and Rush, 2020), a distilled version of the BART sequence-to-sequence model. BART (Lewis et al., 2020) pre-trains by corrupting text with various noise functions and learning to reconstruct the original, developing strong capabilities for text generation tasks. The CNN/DailyMail fine-tuning adapts the model for abstractive summarization.

Generated summaries enable rapid assessment of document content without full reading, comparison of document purposes to differentiate topical similarity from actual plagiarism, and enhanced report narratives that contextualize similarity findings. Together, these three analyses transform raw similarity scores into actionable intelligence about the nature and implications of detected matches."""

